# Rule reinforcement loops

Rules teach behavior; **hooks prove** it. One unified loop beats dozens of separate hooks.

## Three enforcement tiers

| Tier | Mechanism | Best for |
|------|-----------|----------|
| **A — stop loop** | `verify-stop.sh` on every turn end | Git clean, secrets, agent trailers, `verify.json` |
| **B — session / edit** | `session-reinforce.sh`, `after-file-edit.sh`, refresh on `stop` | Autonomy setup; `.cursor/auto-context.md` handoff snapshot |
| **C — rules only** | Always-on `.mdc` + footer reminder | Subjective quality (naming, architecture) |

**Do not** add a separate `stop` hook per rule — cost, noise, and loop_limit exhaustion.

## What the stop loop runs today

1. Auto-fix agent `Co-authored-by` + disable attribution config
2. `check_cli_attribution_config`, `check_git_clean`, `check_staged_secrets`, `check_agent_attribution`
3. Commands in `.cursor/verify.json` (attribution, data-first gate, stack tests)

## What tier B runs

- **sessionStart:** `setup-cursor-autonomy.sh` + `verify-cursor-autonomy.sh` — silently; injects context only on failure.
- **afterFileEdit + stop:** `scripts/refresh-auto-context.sh` → `.cursor/auto-context.md` (see `session-handoff.mdc`).
- **beforeShellExecution:** `shell-guard.sh` then `allow-execution.sh` — deny destructive commands; then allow.

## Adding a new always-on rule

1. Write `.cursor/rules/your-rule.mdc`
2. If **machine-checkable** → add to `hooks/lib/checks.sh` or `.cursor/verify.json`
3. Add one line to `rule_reinforcement_footer` in `checks.sh`
4. If needs a script → agent runs it (`agent-executes.mdc`), do not ask the user

## On follow-up messages

Hard gate. `Shell` with `["all"]`. Micro-commit. Fix yourself. No “should I run verify?”

## Feedback: should every rule have its own loop?

**No.** Use tier A only when a check is objective and cheap. Subjective rules stay tier C; over-looping causes slow turns and follow-up fatigue. This template centralizes tier A in `verify-stop.sh` plus tier B at session start.
